1h_input_tokens": 5046}, "output_tokens": 7, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-29T09:04:17.397Z", "request_id": "req_011CgWFz8ZarcULg7cNo8Lm5", "thinking_duration_ms": 38304}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgWFz8sCDk1piHFCYbqdC", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "By my estimates, the best half is candidates 7, 0, 6 and 1. I worked through the rules by hand at forecast levels and didn't simulate the plans day by day, so the order inside each group is uncertain.\n\n**How I compared them**\n\n- **Products that actually arrive:** Product 0 orders from day 26 on and product 1 orders from day 25 on are paid for but never arrive. On the forecast, product 0 demand from day 3 is 422, or about 441 after the 1.046 factor. Product 1 demand from day 5 is 477, or about 499.\n- **Fixed cost per order day:** It's 47 each. Every 4 days (candidates 0 and 5) costs about 330. Every 3 days (2, 3, 6, 7) costs about 420. Every 2 days (candidate 1) costs about 660. Daily ordering (candidate 4) costs about 1270.\n- **Cost of over- and under-ordering:** Unsold product 0 spoils within 4 days, which loses its 12 purchase price. A shortage costs 9 of lost margin plus a 2 penalty. So the best amount is about the expected demand. Product 1 lasts 7 days and leftovers carry over to the next cycle. But a surplus at the end of the horizon is a straight loss of about 10 per unit.\n- **Warehouse capacity (148):** I spot-checked a few delivery days and found no overflow. Candidate 5 might 